The function uses a **two-pointer technique** on rune slices to avoid allocating intermediate filtered strings. It runs in O(n) time with O(1) extra space.

```go
package main

import "unicode"

// IsPalindrome checks if a string is a palindrome, case-insensitive,
// ignoring non-alphanumeric characters.
func IsPalindrome(s string) bool {
	i := 0
	j := len(s) - 1
	runes := []rune(s)

	for i < j {
		left := unicode.ToLower(runes[i])
		right := unicode.ToLower(runes[j])

		// Skip non-alphanumeric on left
		if !unicode.IsLetter(left) && !unicode.IsDigit(left) {
			i++
			continue
		}
		// Skip non-alphanumeric on right
		if !unicode.IsLetter(right) && !unicode.IsDigit(right) {
			j--
			continue
		}
		// Compare
		if left != right {
			return false
		}
		i++
		j--
	}

	return true
}
```

**Key details:**
- Uses `unicode.ToLower` for **case-insensitive** comparison
- Uses `unicode.IsLetter` + `unicode.IsDigit` to **skip non-alphanumeric** characters (punctuation, spaces, etc.)
- Two pointers (`i` from left, `j` from right) converge inward, skipping invalid chars before comparing
- Works with **Unicode/multi-byte** characters by converting to `[]rune`
- Empty string and single-char strings return `true`

